Language: en

00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:03.030 align:start position:0%
 
the traditional public schools  with whom the teachers union have played  politics  have kept these schools closed have  denied these kids the opportunity to  continue their learning  it is shameful and they have got to get  back  into the classroom  that's education secretary betsy devos  on the importance of getting children  back into the classroom  school lockdowns taking a toll on  students mental and physical health this  many months into the pandemic a family  in maine says their 16 year old son  committed suicide  after he said he felt isolated and was  struggling to cope with the pandemic  now a group of parents along with the  organization called  school choice wisconsin are suing the  city of racine  after it closed its public and private  schools  possibly in defiance of a state supreme  court ruling to prevent  such measures let's go deep on this it  affects so many people across the  country they are watching to see what  happens in wisconsin  jim bender is the president of school  choice wisconsin good to see you jim  first of all what what power  did this entity that closed the schools  and racine  say that it has and does it really have  the power to do that  well in wisconsin we've had an ongoing  debate there's really two layers of  health departments there's the state  health department  and then local units of health  department underneath that and the  statutes govern  those two entities differently we are in  front of the supreme court now  for dane county uh home of uw madison  where the local health official closed  down schools in dane county the  supreme court stepped in and said no  only the statewide  uh health officer can do that that's the  legal argument in front of them they put  an injunction on dane county  then racine followed up and said we're  going to close all the schools in racine  and we have gone to court to have the  injunction get extended from dane county  to racine county to say no this is under  review  you can't close schools just because of  a local health order  and surprisingly the local health  department and the city of racine  has ignored the injunction that the  supreme court extended from dane county  to racine county  so now you're talking about schools that  have been operating  in the fall in a very safe manner  arbitrarily closed from now until  sometime into january  again decisions not based necessarily on  science and data  but on politics and other factors and  that's what i want to ask you about i i  do want to get into the politics of this  because the science is not there  right and we're finding out the science  is not there for the outdoor banding  they were trying to do on on the west  coast in l.a county a judge just said  that  the science is not there and and you see  the arbitrary nature of  in one county versus the other whether  they have the power or not  they shut schools down so how do you  fight this what what  what statistics do you use if they're  not going to use science are you  because it's on your side absolutely  right and i think  what all the schools in the state have  been arguing for is a local approach  meaning a school level approach so if i  look at one school renaissance lutheran  they've been open operating safely 250  total students they had 142 of them  in person following mitigation protocols  social distancing masks things were you  know  obviously kept very clean inside the  school they had a handful of positive  cases  but those were students that were  infected outside the school and because  of their cohorts and their mitigation  there was no  transfer of covet inside the stool and  that's all these schools are asking for  is the ability to have a hybrid approach  that fits their student body their  families their workforce  as it goes forward and that's what most  schools have been doing public and  private around the state of wisconsin  when it comes to creating a safe  learning environment  these one-size-fits-all approaches that  are meant  that are meant to close down all the  schools are not serving the kids and  that's the most important part here  besides the academic slide  what you're seeing in a situation in  racine there was a young girl that wrote  an article  and a column to the health department  that said  listen when the schools got shut down  not only were they not allowed into the  safe learning environment of the school  but grandparents had to take over the  process of virtually  and they had 15 students inside a single  home so now you're talking about a  student that's at a larger cohort more  people  inside a home than she would have been  around in her own school  not following social distancing so it  again it's not the science not the  rational thoughts not the data that's  going into this  in racine what you've seen in the last  10 years dramatic advancement of the  racine parental choice program  where now you've got more than 30  percent of the publicly funded students  are in private schools you've seen a  pretty dramatic decline in enrollment in  the local district school  the mayor of racine is very tight with  the local teachers union  we've had multiple occasions of this  happening across the  teachers union influence over cova  decisions  overrides and supersedes and the local  positivity rate  or hospitalization rate data so you've  got this political overtone to this  which is kind of contextually happening  over the last 10 years  but you're not focused on what's for  kids whether it's for families  and done at a very specific level so  it's frustrating for the schools to have  gone  extra efforts to create safe learning  environments  i i see how the politics weave  themselves in there and  and what your fight is next so in racine  you want to be able to say  you can't close the schools there's an  injunction against doing that in  nearby dane county that's a higher level  decision  and maybe in this time there's a window  for kids to go back to school  jim that would be the hope and then  hopefully vaccines come along and we're  in a better stead  after the beginning of the year we can  start to look back at the science  wow you've got a big job it's for the  most important cause it's for our babies  trying to learn in school thank you for  your fight in racine wisconsin keep us  posted  thank you very much

